   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Hi all,</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Tomorrow at 3:10 in Wickson 2124, Brandon Hurr will give a talk on using dplyr’s join operations to work with related data tables. If you can’t make it in person, the talk will be livecast <a href=\"https://plus.google.com/events/ckviuc7f9l58djgsopvi1dpa36c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://plus.google.com/events/ckviuc7f9l58djgsopvi1dpa36c&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467557194000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1e3AS2ZVk8GwMS4-eiRbQx\">here</a>. After the talk, we’ll have the usual work session until 5pm. Here is a short blurb from Brandon, and the attached script is for following along during the talk. </p>\r\n<blockquote style=\"margin:1.2em 0px;border-left-width:4px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(221,221,221);padding:0px 1em;color:rgb(119,119,119);quotes:none\">\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">In the real world, data is often connected, but stored in independent datasets (tables). Join operations in dplyr mimic those in SQL and allow multiple table integration and filtering. I will introduce you to these operations, and show you how to use them with examples (attached).</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">See you tomorrow,</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Michael</p>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:PGRpdiBjbGFzcz0iZ21haWxfZGVmYXVsdCIgc3R5bGU9ImZvbnQtZmFtaWx5OiB0YWhvbWEsIHNh\r\nbnMtc2VyaWY7IGZvbnQtc2l6ZTogc21hbGw7Ij5IaSBhbGwsPC9kaXY+PGRpdiBjbGFzcz0iZ21h\r\naWxfZGVmYXVsdCIgc3R5bGU9ImZvbnQtZmFtaWx5OiB0YWhvbWEsIHNhbnMtc2VyaWY7IGZvbnQt\r\nc2l6ZTogc21hbGw7Ij48YnI+PC9kaXY+PGRpdiBjbGFzcz0iZ21haWxfZGVmYXVsdCIgc3R5bGU9\r\nImZvbnQtZmFtaWx5OiB0YWhvbWEsIHNhbnMtc2VyaWY7IGZvbnQtc2l6ZTogc21hbGw7Ij5Ub21v\r\ncnJvdyBhdCAzOjEwIGluIFdpY2tzb24gMjEyNCwgQnJhbmRvbiBIdXJyIHdpbGwgZ2l2ZSBhIHRh\r\nbGsgb24gdXNpbmcgZHBseXIncyBqb2luIG9wZXJhdGlvbnMgdG8gd29yayB3